    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **36 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds per-country national-number length validation to E.164 telephone normalization.\n\n**File modified:** `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidateOwnerFields.java`\n\nThe telephone normalizer (`toE164`) already stripped separators, inferred `+61` for national numbers, and enforced a generic 8\u201315 total-digit bound. I added a national-length check on top:\n\n- A `NATIONAL_LENGTH_BY_COUNTRY` map pins the required national-digit count per country code: `+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10.\n- A new `hasValidNationalLength` helper strips the country code from the leading digits and requires the remaining national digits to match exactly for a known code. Unknown country codes (e.g. `+64`) fall through to the existing generic rule, so nothing else regresses.\n- When the national length is wrong, `toE164` returns `null`, which surfaces as a `telephone` field error \u2192 **400**, consistent with the existing rejection path.\n\nThis makes `Cp26Tests.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 3 national digits, needs 9 \u2192 400) pass, while the other telephone checkpoints remain green:\n- `+64 21 123 456` (NZ, unknown code) still accepted (Cp08)\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` (9 national digits) still accepted (Cp08, Cp03)\n- The base `uniqueTelephone()` `04XXXXXXXX` payload (9 national digits under `+61`) stays valid across all checkpoints.",
